<h2 id="who">Who runs this site</h2>
<p>Tide Hours is run by one person, Chelsea Kelly-Reif, in California. You can reach me at <a href="mailto:chelsea@chelseakr.com">chelsea@chelseakr.com</a>.</p>

<h2 id="what">What this site is, and is not</h2>
<p>The site shows tide predictions that it computes itself from public-domain NOAA CO-OPS data: harmonic constants, datums and station offsets. It is not a NOAA product, and it is not affiliated with or endorsed by NOAA or any other agency.</p>
<p><strong>The predictions are not for navigation.</strong> They are predicted astronomical tides, not observations or forecasts. Wind, storms, river flow and air pressure can push the real water well above or below them. Do not rely on this site for boating, anchoring, crossing a bar, walking out on a beach or tide flat, or any other decision where a wrong time or height could hurt someone. For those, use NOAA's own products, nautical charts, National Weather Service forecasts and warnings, posted signs and local knowledge.</p>

<h2 id="data">The predictions can be wrong</h2>
<p>The site's tests compare its predictions with NOAA's own published predictions, and the <a href="/about/">about page</a> says how closely they match. They can still differ from NOAA's, NOAA's source data can change or contain errors, a station's corrections are only estimates, and a bug can make a page wrong. The site is rebuilt on a schedule, so a page can be out of date.</p>

